---
import PortfolioEntry from '../components/PortfolioEntry.astro';
import YearRange from '../components/YearRange.astro';
import LayoutGlobal from '../layouts/LayoutGlobal.astro';
import { profile } from '../utilities/microdata.ts';
import { asCanonicalUrl } from '../utilities/urls.ts';
---

<LayoutGlobal
	meta={{
		title: "Adam Coster's Portfolio",
		description:
			"The things that Adam Coster has built and done over the years. Perhaps you'd call it a portfolio. Or a curriculum vitae. Maybe a résumé?",
		canonical: '/',
		ldjsons: [profile],
		social: { image: asCanonicalUrl('/profile.portrait.1372.jpg') },
	}}
>
	<h1>My Portfolio</h1>

	<p>
		I don't have an extensive <abbr title="curriculum vitae">CV</abbr> since, hey,
		I'm (co-)running my own show so I'm not on the job market. But there are all kinds
		of reasons someone might want to see my resumé/portfolio, so here ya go!
	</p>

	<h2>A brief timeline</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<YearRange from="2014" />: Co-Founder and, variably, CTO or CEO at
			<a href="https://www.bscotch.net">Butterscotch Shenanigans</a> (a.k.a. "Bscotch").
			See the next section for details.
		</li>
		<li>
			<YearRange from="2010" to="2014" />: Ph.D. in Cellular & Molecular Biology
			at University of Texas Southwestern ("UT Southwestern"). My research lived
			at the intersection of wet-lab biology and computational biology. In the
			unlikely scenario you want to know more about that, check out <a
				href="https://github.com/adam-coster/dissertation">my dissertation</a
			> (it has the incredibly approachable title: "Quantitative single-cell imaging
			reveals insulation of morphogenic signal transduction").
		</li>
		<li>
			<YearRange from={2007} to={2010} />: Various lab technician and teaching
			assistant roles at UChicago and Washington University ("WashU").
		</li>
		<li>
			<YearRange from={2004} to={2007} />: B.A. in Biology from the University
			of Chicago ("UChicago").
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>My work at Bscotch</h2>

	<p>
		My brothers and I co-run the studio and make all high-level business and
		management decisions together. So I collaborate with them on all of that
		big-picture "BizDev" stuff: reviewing/negotiating contracts, analyzing data,
		interpreting market signals, managing our team, project planning and
		management, wrangling 3rd parties, and on and on. Whatever needs doing to
		keep the machine running.
	</p>

	<p>
		But that's the background stuff and not really what I consider my primary
		work in the studio. while my brothers have always focused on the game design
		and development side of things, I've mostly been in the background doing
		what I call "Productivity Engineering": figuring out how to make the work of
		game development and publishing more efficient, more fun, and more reliable.
		That's mostly been via the web platform.
	</p>

	<p>
		So I've done many things, and we've been fortunate to still be kicking after
		over a decade in this <i>extremely</i> tumultuous industry. Most of that needs
		way too much context to make interesting, so the following is just the short list
		of what (I think) are the most interesting projects I've worked on at Bscotch.
	</p>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<PortfolioEntry title="Rumpus" from="2017" to="2020">
				<p>
					BscotchID quickly became difficult to expand and maintain, since I had
					no idea what I was doing when I first built it and it got quite messy.
					So I took a bunch of time to learn the basics of the popular web
					frameworks and web infrastructure strategies of the day (oh how
					quickly they change) and set off to do it "right".
				</p>
				<p>
					And so I built "Rumpus", which eventually subsumed BscotchID and
					became the web backbone of the studio. It includes a collection of
					data storage models built for game-specific needs (made general enough
					that the needs of any of our games would be covered) all in service of
					store- and platform-agnostic player data syncing, integrations with
					authentication and licensing for every store and platform we sell
					games on, an automated system for publishing release notes, and lots
					of features for internal use in game development.
				</p>
				<p>
					Rumpus is used in all of our games, stores well over a million user
					accounts, and handles millions of web requests per month.
				</p>
			</PortfolioEntry>
			<li>
				<PortfolioEntry title="Crashlands Creator" from="2015" to="2016">
					<p>
						An internal, web-based editor for creating the map and story content
						for Crashlands. We also considered opening this up to players
						someday to allow them to create their own stories in Crashlands, but
						ultimately (and thankfully, in retrospect) decided against it.
					</p>
				</PortfolioEntry>
				<li>
					<PortfolioEntry title="InkPipe v0" from="2015" to="2016">
						<p>
							We were making our games in GameMaker, with art assets made in
							Inkscape. The manual process of exporting images from Inkscape and
							then into GameMaker was tedious and error-prone, and we also
							needed some light image processing to remove aliasing artifacts
							when sprites were drawn in subpixel positions or at weird scales.
							So I slapped together an internal pipeline to handle all of that,
							so that the artist could make the art and the game programmer
							could have it automatically updated in-game without any manual
							steps.
						</p>
					</PortfolioEntry>
				</li>
				<li>
					<PortfolioEntry title="BscotchID" from="2014" to="2017">
						<p>
							The first version of my cross-platform user account service (later
							replaced with "Rumpus"). It included cross-platform save syncing,
							cross-platform achievements (with cross-game content unlocks), a
							bespoke newsletter service, and some other neat stuff. Despite
							being my first foray into web development, BscotchID worked quite
							well and wasn't fully taken offline until 2026.
						</p>
					</PortfolioEntry>
				</li>
			</li>
		</li>
	</ul>
</LayoutGlobal>
